From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timon Stipkovits <timon2201@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 18:55:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] mm/vmscan: let global direct reclaim leave THP-splitting
 shrinkers to kswapd

Two shrinkers free memory only by splitting transparent huge pages:

- thp-deferred_split (deferred_split_scan()) splits partially unmapped
  THPs, and, when khugepaged/max_ptes_none is below 511, THPs that
  thp_underused() finds to be mostly zero pages. That check compares
  the pages of each queued folio with the zero page one by one.

- the tmpfs/shmem part of the superblock shrinker
  (shmem_unused_huge_scan()) splits huge pages that reach past the end
  of their file. With shmem_enabled=always every small memfd written
  with write() or fallocate() has one.

Both run from shrink_slab() in whichever task reclaims, including a
game thread in direct reclaim. Splitting a mapped huge page unmaps and
remaps it and flushes the TLB of the CPUs running that process. The
task itself needs a few dozen pages, and kswapd, which the allocator
woke before it entered direct reclaim, can do the splitting. This is
the reasoning of 0008 (split instead of compressing 2 MiB in direct
reclaim) and 0009 (leave optional MGLRU aging to kswapd), applied to
the shrinkers.

Add vm.thp_shrinker_defer_direct. When set, prepare_shrink_slab()
marks the reclaim_state of a global direct reclaim pass, and both
shrinkers check reclaim_defers_thp_split(): deferred_split_scan()
returns SHRINK_STOP, so do_shrink_slab() carries the work over in
nr_deferred and the next call (normally kswapd's) scans that much more;
shmem_unused_huge_scan() reports nothing freed, because
super_cache_scan() adds its return value to what the dentry and inode
caches freed.

do_shrink_slab() scales a shrinker's work by freeable >> priority. With
the default batch of 128, the deferred split shrinker runs at priority
12 only with about 260 000 queued THPs, and at priority 6 with about
4 000. In practice these shrinkers therefore run in direct reclaim only
after the priority has dropped, when that reclaim is already slow.
Deferring at the first priorities only would do nothing. Defer at every
priority except the last two passes (1 and 0), so the memory held by
those huge pages still counts before the allocator decides on retries
or OOM. Memcg reclaim (no kswapd to defer to), proactive reclaim and
kswapd are unchanged.

Not measured yet. Check first whether these shrinkers show up in game
or audio threads at all: tools/shrinkwho.bt times both (thp-split and
shmem-split, key 0 = not kswapd). The cost of deferring is
that direct reclaim may evict other memory instead; compare
thp_split_page, thp_deferred_split_page, pswpout and
workingset_refault_* with the sysctl at 0 and 1.

Default off.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.rst | 24 +++++++++++++
 include/linux/swap.h                    | 19 ++++++++++
 mm/huge_memory.c                        |  9 +++++
 mm/shmem.c                              | 10 ++++++
 mm/vmscan.c                             | 46 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 5 files changed, 108 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.rst b/Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.rst
index fd5b9644d..de31a0309 100644
--- a/Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.rst
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.rst
@@ -74,6 +74,7 @@ Currently, these files are in /proc/sys/vm:
 - numa_stat
 - swappiness
 - thp_direct_reclaim_split
+- thp_shrinker_defer_direct
 - unprivileged_userfaultfd
 - user_reserve_kbytes
 - vfs_cache_pressure
@@ -1176,6 +1177,29 @@ thp_swpout_direct_split in /proc/vmstat counts the splits.
 The default value is 0.
 
 
+thp_shrinker_defer_direct
+=========================
+
+When set to 1, global direct reclaim (an allocating task reclaiming
+memory itself) skips the two shrinkers that free memory only by splitting
+transparent huge pages, and leaves them to kswapd: the deferred split
+queue (partially unmapped huge pages, and huge pages that are mostly zero
+pages when khugepaged/max_ptes_none is below 511) and tmpfs/shmem (huge
+pages that reach past the end of their file). In the last two passes of
+a direct reclaim attempt (reclaim priority 1 and 0) it runs them as
+before, so that memory still counts before an allocation fails. Memory
+cgroup reclaim, proactive reclaim (memory.reclaim) and kswapd are not
+affected.
+
+Splitting a mapped huge page unmaps and remaps it and flushes the TLB of
+the CPUs running that process, in the task that happened to reclaim. Leaving
+the splits to kswapd can make direct reclaim take other memory instead;
+compare thp_split_page, thp_deferred_split_page, pswpout and
+workingset_refault_* in /proc/vmstat when changing this.
+
+The default value is 0.
+
+
 unprivileged_userfaultfd
 ========================
 
diff --git a/include/linux/swap.h b/include/linux/swap.h
index a483c7b38..273755d40 100644
--- a/include/linux/swap.h
+++ b/include/linux/swap.h
@@ -150,8 +150,27 @@ struct reclaim_state {
 	/* per-thread mm walk data */
 	struct lru_gen_mm_walk *mm_walk;
 #endif
+	/*
+	 * Set by reclaim before it runs the shrinkers: shrinkers that free
+	 * memory only by splitting huge pages leave that to kswapd, see
+	 * reclaim_defers_thp_split().
+	 */
+	bool defer_thp_split;
 };
 
+/*
+ * reclaim_defers_thp_split(): should a shrinker that frees memory by splitting
+ * huge pages skip this call?
+ *
+ * True in global direct reclaim, except in its last two passes, when
+ * vm.thp_shrinker_defer_direct is set; such a shrinker then returns
+ * SHRINK_STOP (or reports nothing freed) and its work is left to kswapd.
+ */
+static inline bool reclaim_defers_thp_split(void)
+{
+	return current->reclaim_state && current->reclaim_state->defer_thp_split;
+}
+
 /*
  * mm_account_reclaimed_pages(): account reclaimed pages outside of LRU-based
  * reclaim
diff --git a/mm/huge_memory.c b/mm/huge_memory.c
index 1e5d68acf..95aadde96 100644
--- a/mm/huge_memory.c
+++ b/mm/huge_memory.c
@@ -4661,6 +4661,15 @@ static unsigned long deferred_split_scan(struct shrinker *shrink,
 	int split = 0;
 	unsigned long isolated;
 
+	/*
+	 * Splitting is the only way this shrinker frees memory, and it is
+	 * expensive for the task that does it. Global direct reclaim may leave
+	 * it to kswapd; SHRINK_STOP keeps the work counted as deferred, so the
+	 * next call (normally kswapd's) scans that much more.
+	 */
+	if (reclaim_defers_thp_split())
+		return SHRINK_STOP;
+
 	isolated = list_lru_shrink_walk_irq(&deferred_split_lru, sc,
 					    deferred_split_isolate, &dispose);
 
diff --git a/mm/shmem.c b/mm/shmem.c
index 848316eaa..86c2b52ef 100644
--- a/mm/shmem.c
+++ b/mm/shmem.c
@@ -840,6 +840,16 @@ static long shmem_unused_huge_scan(struct super_block *sb,
 	if (!READ_ONCE(sbinfo->shrinklist_len))
 		return SHRINK_STOP;
 
+	/*
+	 * This frees memory only by splitting huge pages beyond i_size; global
+	 * direct reclaim may leave that to kswapd (see
+	 * reclaim_defers_thp_split()). Report nothing freed rather than
+	 * SHRINK_STOP: super_cache_scan() adds the return value to what the
+	 * dentry and inode caches freed.
+	 */
+	if (reclaim_defers_thp_split())
+		return 0;
+
 	return shmem_unused_huge_shrink(sbinfo, sc, 0);
 }
 
diff --git a/mm/vmscan.c b/mm/vmscan.c
index d3a7ef016..d905fecc5 100644
--- a/mm/vmscan.c
+++ b/mm/vmscan.c
@@ -1095,6 +1095,41 @@ static int sysctl_thp_direct_reclaim_split __read_mostly;
  */
 static int sysctl_lru_gen_defer_direct_aging __read_mostly;
 
+/*
+ * vm.thp_shrinker_defer_direct: global direct reclaim leaves the shrinkers
+ * that free memory only by splitting huge pages to kswapd, see
+ * prepare_shrink_slab().
+ */
+static int sysctl_thp_shrinker_defer_direct __read_mostly;
+
+/*
+ * Called before each shrink_slab() of a reclaim pass. Two shrinkers free
+ * memory only by splitting huge pages: the deferred split queue (partially
+ * mapped THPs, and THPs found to be mostly zero pages when
+ * khugepaged/max_ptes_none is below 511) and tmpfs/shmem (huge pages that
+ * reach past the end of their file). Splitting a mapped huge page unmaps and
+ * remaps it and flushes the TLB of the CPUs running that process, and the
+ * underused check compares the pages of a folio with the zero page one by one,
+ * all in the task that happened to reclaim. That task needs a few dozen pages,
+ * and kswapd, which the allocator woke before direct reclaim started, can do
+ * this work instead.
+ *
+ * do_shrink_slab() scales the work by freeable >> priority, so at the first
+ * priorities these shrinkers rarely run at all; they show up in direct reclaim
+ * once the priority has dropped, which is also when that reclaim already takes
+ * long. Only global direct reclaim defers, and not in the last two passes
+ * (priority 1 and 0): before the allocator gives up, the memory held by those
+ * huge pages counts again. Memcg reclaim (no kswapd to defer to) and proactive
+ * reclaim (not latency sensitive) are unchanged.
+ */
+static void prepare_shrink_slab(struct scan_control *sc)
+{
+	sc->reclaim_state.defer_thp_split =
+		READ_ONCE(sysctl_thp_shrinker_defer_direct) &&
+		!current_is_kswapd() && root_reclaim(sc) && !sc->proactive &&
+		sc->priority > 1;
+}
+
 /*
  * Swapping out a large anon folio to zram (or any synchronous swap device,
  * or zswap) compresses every page of it in the reclaiming task: 3-4 ms for
@@ -5239,6 +5274,7 @@ static int shrink_one(struct lruvec *lruvec, struct scan_control *sc)
 
 	need_rotate = try_to_shrink_lruvec(lruvec, sc);
 
+	prepare_shrink_slab(sc);
 	shrink_slab(sc->gfp_mask, pgdat->node_id, memcg, sc->priority);
 
 	if (!sc->proactive)
@@ -6349,6 +6385,7 @@ static void shrink_node_memcgs(pg_data_t *pgdat, struct scan_control *sc)
 
 		shrink_lruvec(lruvec, sc);
 
+		prepare_shrink_slab(sc);
 		shrink_slab(sc->gfp_mask, pgdat->node_id, memcg,
 			    sc->priority);
 
@@ -7883,6 +7920,15 @@ static const struct ctl_table vmscan_sysctl_table[] = {
 		.extra1		= SYSCTL_ZERO,
 		.extra2		= SYSCTL_ONE,
 	},
+	{
+		.procname	= "thp_shrinker_defer_direct",
+		.data		= &sysctl_thp_shrinker_defer_direct,
+		.maxlen		= sizeof(sysctl_thp_shrinker_defer_direct),
+		.mode		= 0644,
+		.proc_handler	= proc_dointvec_minmax,
+		.extra1		= SYSCTL_ZERO,
+		.extra2		= SYSCTL_ONE,
+	},
 #ifdef CONFIG_NUMA
 	{
 		.procname	= "zone_reclaim_mode",
